rve $C$ is a quarter of a circle of radius $3$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}3\\operatorname{sin}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{3x}{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{3(3\\operatorname{cos}t)}{3\\operatorname{sin}t} \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cot}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }3\\operatorname{cot}t\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{sin}t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-9\\operatorname{sin}t{|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-9[\\operatorname{sin}\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)] \\\\  & =-9(0-1) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186259619 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}t,\\hspace{0.222em}4\\operatorname{sin}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}\\pi \\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{y{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}}{8x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{